-- title:
--   Stored ancestor lists match tree paths
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Consider the candidate stages and the extracted tree.
--
--   1. **Ancestor lists.** The ancestor list of a child is its parent's list followed by the parent's map, exactly the recursion of the path displacement.
--   2. **Agreement.** For every node $v$ and point $z$, the displacement summed over the stored list equals the path displacement:
--   $$
--   \sum_{h\in\mathrm{anc}(v)}\|h(z)-z\|=\Delta(v,z).
--   $$
--   The ancestor sum checked when a candidate is selected is therefore the final tree's proper-ancestor displacement of the new child.
